{"id":"84fd2ad8d1c2","content":"3 min read\nJust now\n\n--\n\nDay 3: Why Do We Still Need HTML & CSS in the Age of React?\nPress enter or click to view image in full size\n\nFigure 1: The three building blocks of every website — HTML provides the structure, CSS defines the presentation, and JavaScript adds interactivity. Modern frontend frameworks are built on top of these core technologies.Yesterday, I understood how browsers and servers communicate using HTTP and HTTPS.\nToday, I wanted to answer another question that had been sitting in my mind for a long time.\nIf React, Angular, Vue, AI tools, and modern frameworks exist, why do we still need HTML and CSS?\nFor a long time, I thought frameworks had somehow replaced them.\nAfter revisiting the fundamentals, I realized something important.\nThey haven’t replaced HTML and CSS — they’re built on top of them.\nThat realisation completely changed how I looked at frontend development.\nHTML — The Structure of Every Webpage\nHTML (HyperText Markup Language) is the foundation of every webpage.\nIts responsibility isn’t to make a website beautiful or interactive.\nIts job is to define what each piece of content actually is.\nFor example,\n\n• <h1> → Main Heading\n\n• <p> → Paragraph\n\n• <img> → Image\n\n• <a> → Hyperlink\n\n• <button> → Button\n\n• <input> → Input field\n\n• <form> → Form\n\n• <table> → Table\n\n• <ul> & <ol> → Lists\nWithout HTML, the browser would simply have text with no understanding of what that text represents.\nHTML Is More Than Just Tags\nOne thing I had underestimated was Semantic HTML.\nEarlier, I used to think that using <div> everywhere was enough.\nBut HTML provides meaningful elements like:\n\n• <header>\n\n• <nav>\n\n• <main>\n\n• <section>\n\n• <article>\n\n• <aside>\n\n• <footer>\nThese tags don’t just make the code easier to read.\nThey also improve:\n\n• Accessibility\n\n• SEO\n\n• Code maintainability\nThe browser, search engines, and screen readers all understand the page much better when semantic elements are used correctly.\nForms — The Way Users Talk to Websites\nAlmost every website needs user input.\nWhether it’s:\n\n• Login\n\n• Signup\n\n• Search\n\n• Contact Us\n\n• Payment\nEverything starts with HTML forms.\nSome common elements include:\n\n• <input>\n\n• <textarea>\n\n• <select>\n\n• <label>\n\n• <button>\n\n• <form>\nI also revisited why labels and input types matter — not just for usability but also for accessibility.\nCSS — Giving Life to HTML\nIf HTML creates the structure, CSS (Cascading Style Sheets) decides how everything looks.\nCSS controls almost everything we see on a webpage.\nFor example,\n\n• Colors\n\n• Fonts\n\n• Backgrounds\n\n• Borders\n\n• Shadows\n\n• Spacing\n\n• Width & Height\n\n• Animations\n\n• Responsive layouts\nWithout CSS, every website would look like a plain document.\nSome CSS Concepts That Finally Made Sense\nInstead of memorizing properties, I started understanding why they exist.\nSome of the concepts I revisited were:\n\n• Selectors\n\n• Classes & IDs\n\n• Box Model\n\n• Margin\n\n• Padding\n\n• Border\n\n• Display\n\n• Position\n\n• Flexbox\n\n• Grid\n\n• Media Queries\nOne concept that I found especially interesting was the Box Model.\nEvery HTML element is actually treated as a box consisting of:\n\n• Content\n\n• Padding\n\n• Border\n\n• Margin\nUnderstanding this explains why spacing behaves the way it does.\nResponsive Design\nOne thing that’s easy to forget is that users don’t all have the same screen size.\nA website that looks perfect on a laptop may break completely on a mobile device.\nThat’s where responsive design comes in.\nUsing media queries, flexible layouts, and relative units helps websites adapt to different screen sizes instead of creating separate websites for each device.\nMy Biggest Realisation\nThe biggest lesson today wasn’t about remembering HTML tags or CSS properties.\nIt was understanding their responsibilities.\n\n• HTML provides the structure.\n\n• CSS handles presentation.\n\n• JavaScript handles behavior.\nReact doesn’t replace HTML.\nJSX eventually becomes HTML.\nSimilarly, React doesn’t replace CSS — it simply provides different ways to organise and write styles.\nThe fundamentals remain exactly the same.\nAnd I think that’s something many developers (including me) sometimes overlook while learning frameworks.\nWhat’s Next?\nNow that I’ve revisited the building blocks of every webpage, it’s finally time to learn the language that makes websites interactive.\nIn the next article, I’ll begin revisiting JavaScript from scratch — not by memorizing syntax, but by understanding why every concept exists and how to think like a programmer.\nI’m excited to see where this journey takes me.\nIf you’re also revisiting the fundamentals or strengthening your software engineering knowledge, I’d love to have you along on this journey.\n“The more I revisit the fundamentals, the more I realise that frameworks change, but the building blocks of the web remain the same.”\nIf you have any suggestions or feedback, feel free to reach out at urbashijrall3003@gmail.com."}